#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ruflo-item -- Ruflo memory and gates around one meaningful feature bundle.

  ruflo-item start  <ID> "<task>"           hooks pre-task, memory search, hooks route, memory store
  ruflo-item start  --item ID:TEXT [--item ID:TEXT ...]
                                             several ids, each with its own task text, in one invocation
  ruflo-item note   <ID> "<text>"           memory store, keyed to the item, timestamped from the real clock
  ruflo-item ready  <ID> [<ID> ...] "<summary>"
                                             quick gate once; no post-task or testgaps
  ruflo-item checkpoint <ID> [<ID> ...] "<summary>"
                                             merge gate once; checkpoint memory and testgaps once
  ruflo-item land   <ID> [<ID> ...] "<summary>"
                                             release gate; post-task, done memory and testgaps once
  ruflo-item done   ...                      compatibility alias for checkpoint
  ruflo-item recall "<keywords>"            memory list, then memory search
  ruflo-item from-ledger <LEDGER.json> [--since YYYY-MM-DD]
                                             record start+done for every item closed on or after --since
                                             (or every done item, with no --since), from one ledger file
  ruflo-item ... --project DIR              default: `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` of the current directory
  ruflo-item ... --namespace NS             default: $RUFLO_NAMESPACE, else the project directory name

  Example (a lead closing a batch): after landing O-11, O-12 and O-13 in one
  sweep, instead of three cold-started `done` calls --

    ruflo-item done --item O-11:"batch start/done, from-ledger backfill mode" \\
                     --item O-12:"workflow.html stamp now generated" \\
                     --item O-13:"routing table dedup"

  -- one process, one daemon start, one stop, the merge gate run once.

Every transition accepts one or more item ids followed by one shared summary,
or repeatable `--item ID:TEXT`. Gates run once per bundle. Green receipts are
reused only at the same verification level with the same command and exact
worktree bytes; checkpoint evidence never certifies release. Only
`checkpoint`/legacy `done` and `land` dispatch `testgaps`.

BATCHING (proposal 31, O-11 / finding 31/F-05). Every Ruflo call auto-starts
its daemon and every `ruflo-item` invocation stops it again in a `finally`
(see "WHY THE DAEMON STOPS ON EVERY EXIT" below) -- correctly, but it means
the *next* invocation cold-starts it again: one call measured 3.3s, and ten
items recorded one at a time (twenty invocations) did not finish in 500s.
`start` and `done` each accept several ids in a *single* invocation via a
repeatable `--item ID:TEXT` flag (split on the first `:`, so TEXT itself may
contain colons) instead of the positional `<ID> "<task>"` /
`<ID> [<ID> ...] "<summary>"` form -- one process, one binary resolution, one
daemon start and one stop for the whole bundle, whatever else changes. A
plain positional call with exactly one id (today's shape) behaves exactly as
before; `--item` and the positional form are mutually exclusive within one
call. `done --item ...` still runs the merge gate exactly once for the
bundle, before any store, same as the positional multi-id form.

Each id in a batch is independent: a failing id (its own `memory store`
failing) is reported on stderr and does not stop the rest of the batch from
being recorded -- `start`/`done` exit nonzero if *any* id in the call failed,
but every id that succeeded is still stored. `done` skips its final testgaps
dispatch when any id in the batch failed, matching single-id behaviour today
(a failed store there already means "no testgaps"). The daemon still stops
exactly once, in the same `finally`, on a clean run, a partial failure, a
crash, or a signal -- batching never adds a second start or a second stop.

`from-ledger LEDGER [--since DATE]` is the same idea driven by a ledger file
instead of typing ids by hand: proposal 31, O-11's own backfill needed
fourteen `start`+`done` pairs for items already shipped, and doing that one
call at a time (28 invocations) took about eight minutes of cold starts.
`from-ledger` loads LEDGER once, finds every item with status "done" whose
closed date (`tools/tracker/ledger.py`'s `closed_date` -- the same reading
`bin/conformance` check 9 uses) is on or after `--since` (every done item,
with no `--since`), and for each one stores `item:<ID>:start` and
`item:<ID>:done` directly, using the item's own `title` as the text. This is
a memory *record* of history, not a re-run of history: it does **not**
re-run the project's merge gate (the code shipped and was gated long ago;
re-running it now proves nothing new and would dominate the very cold-start
cost this mode exists to avoid), and it does not run `hooks pre-task` /
`route` / `post-task` / the testgaps dispatch, which are live-work signals
that would misrepresent when the work actually happened. Every stored value
says outright that it was backfilled and when, e.g. "... -- backfilled
2026-09-18T09:00:00Z from 31-....json, originally closed 2026-09-17;
recorded after the fact, not logged as the work happened" -- so nothing
reading Ruflo memory later can mistake this for a live start/done pair. Like
`start`/`done`, a failing id is reported and does not stop the rest; the
call exits nonzero if any id failed, and the daemon still stops exactly
once.

This lifts PhotoVault app's own `tools/ruflo_task.sh` into common-rules, so
every project gets the same four commands instead of the four steps as prose
in templates/lead-prompt.md SS2 -- proposal 19's build had one agent's loop
error with nothing noticing, and the engine's Ruflo ledger recorded 0 usable
memory hits across 43 agents who were doing the four steps by hand. `start`
also stores `item:<ID>:start`: the app's own CLAUDE.md rule is "an item
without start and done in Ruflo memory is not done", which a start that only
called `hooks pre-task` could never satisfy.

WHY `done` RUNS THE GATE FIRST. `hooks post-task --success true` and a
`memory store` are how the rest of the project learns an item is finished;
recording that before the project's own test gate has run would let a broken
item read as done everywhere Ruflo is asked. So `done` calls
`tools/project.py`'s `test_command()` -- the exact function `bin/land` answers
its own gate question with -- runs it, and only calls `hooks post-task` and
`memory store` once it is green. A gate that refuses (a `.common-rules.json`
`false  # <why>` line, same as `bin/land` prints) is a failure like any
other: no post-task, no store, and ruflo-item exits non-zero. When no gate is
declared and none can be guessed (`test_command()` answers ""), that is not
"nothing to run" -- it is the same "cannot verify, so refuse" `bin/land`
enforces, and `done` refuses the same way rather than recording success on
top of zero evidence. The gate's own stdout/stderr are never captured -- they
stream live, inherited from ruflo-item's own stdio, exactly as they would
running directly in a terminal; only the exit code decides pass or fail.
After a green gate and its post-task/store, `done` also runs
`hooks worker dispatch --trigger testgaps`, best-effort, matching the app's
own `tools/ruflo_task.sh`, whose CLAUDE.md depends on it running.

WHY THE DAEMON STOPS ON EVERY EXIT. Every Ruflo CLI call auto-starts its
daemon, which spawns headless Claude sessions that burn tokens until told to
stop (measured in the PhotoVault engine, 7 September; see bin/warmup). So
`<binary> daemon stop` runs in a `finally`, after a clean run, after a
refused or failing gate, after a CLI error mid-command, and after
KeyboardInterrupt, SIGTERM or SIGHUP -- never `pkill` or `killall`, which
this repo's own rules forbid killing by name. A SIGTERM or SIGHUP received
while `done`'s gate is running also terminates the gate's whole process
group first (it runs in its own session, started with `start_new_session`,
precisely so that group can be signalled as a unit) -- otherwise the gate's
own children would be orphaned when ruflo-item exits. The same applies to a
plain Ctrl-C (`KeyboardInterrupt`), for the same reason. Exit code on a
caught terminating signal is `128 + signum` (143 for SIGTERM, 129 for
SIGHUP), the same convention a shell uses for a killed job.

BINARY RESOLUTION (RF-01: tools/ruflo.py, shared with bin/warmup and bin/
conformance -- never two copies of this logic). `$RUFLO`, if set, is a full
command line, split with `shlex` -- so `RUFLO="npx -y ruflo@latest"` (the
app's own wrapper's call) works, and its first word must resolve to
something executable (via `shutil.which`) or ruflo-item refuses, naming the
word, rather than handing a command to `subprocess` that cannot run. Then
`claude-flow` on PATH, then `ruflo` on PATH. Then the npx cache
(`~/.npm/_npx/*/node_modules/.bin/{claude-flow,ruflo}`), picking the highest
version by each candidate's own package.json, never mtime or glob order --
this machine's cache alone has held several versions at once. Only `$RUFLO`
may ever name `npx` -- ruflo-item never adds it on its own initiative, since
a bare `npx` call downloads a package and auto-starts the daemon on its own.
No binary resolves: say so, naming all three places searched, name the fix
(`set RUFLO, e.g. RUFLO="npx -y ruflo@latest"`), exit 2 -- there is nothing
to call `daemon stop` on, so that step is skipped, not attempted. The binary
actually used is printed on every run, like the namespace already is.

NAMESPACE (tools/ruflo.py). `--namespace NS` when given, else
`$RUFLO_NAMESPACE`, else `.common-rules.json`'s `ruflo_namespace` key
(tools/project.py), else the project directory's own name (the app's
wrapper used one fixed namespace, `photovault-app`; ruflo-item serves every
project, so it needs one it can derive without being told, but a wrong
guess must be visible rather than silently losing `recall`'s history -- so
the namespace in use is printed on every run).

RUFLO STATE CWD (proposal 23, F-03). `.swarm/` and whatever else Ruflo keeps
as state exist only in the main checkout -- a linked worktree's own root is
the wrong cwd for every Ruflo call, and every one of them used to fail there
with "Database not initialized" while the item's own work (the gate, in
`done`) ran fine, so nothing outwardly looked wrong. `ruflo_cwd_for()`
resolves `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`'s parent -- the main checkout's own
root -- and every `hooks`/`memory`/`daemon` call below runs there, while the
gate itself still runs in `project` (the worktree actually being worked in).
In the main checkout the two paths are the same, so behaviour is unchanged
there. And because a Ruflo call silently failing was exactly F-03's
complaint, the `memory store` call that actually persists
`item:<ID>:start`/`item:<ID>:done`/`item:<ID>:note` is no longer best-effort:
its failure now exits ruflo-item nonzero with a one-line message, rather than
being folded into the same `|| true` telemetry as pre-task/route/search.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 the gate refused or failed, a required memory store
failed for one or more ids in the call (or a bad done/note/recall/from-ledger
argument), 2 no Ruflo binary found or a malformed CLI invocation,
128+signum on SIGTERM/SIGHUP (143/129),
130 on Ctrl-C.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import os
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

RULES_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(RULES_DIR))

from tools import project as P  # noqa: E402
from tools import ruflo as RU  # noqa: E402
from tools import gate_receipts as GR  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import ledger as LED  # noqa: E402

PREFIX = "ruflo-item:"


class _Signaled(Exception):
    """Raised from the SIGTERM/SIGHUP handler so ordinary try/finally and
    except clauses see it exactly like any other exception -- including
    KeyboardInterrupt, which needs the identical gate-cleanup treatment."""

    def __init__(self, signum: int):
        super().__init__(signum)
        self.signum = signum


def _install_signal_handlers() -> None:
    def handler(signum: int, _frame) -> None:
        raise _Signaled(signum)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, handler)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGHUP, handler)


def resolve_binary() -> tuple[list[str] | None, str | None]:
    """(argv prefix, None) on success; (None, the message to print, prefixed)
    when nothing usable resolves. tools/ruflo.py does the actual resolution
    (RF-01), shared with bin/warmup and bin/conformance -- never two copies."""
    argv, err = RU.resolve_binary()
    if argv is None:
        return None, f"{PREFIX} {err}"
    return argv, None


def project_root(explicit: str | None) -> Path:
    """--project DIR when given; otherwise `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` of
    the current directory, falling back to the current directory itself when
    that is not a git repository at all (a scratch project with no VCS yet)."""
    if explicit:
        return Path(explicit).resolve()
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
                        capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=str(Path.cwd()))
    if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip():
        return Path(r.stdout.strip()).resolve()
    return Path.cwd().resolve()


def ruflo_cwd_for(project: Path) -> Path:
    """Where every Ruflo call (and `daemon stop`) should run -- proposal 23
    F-03: `.swarm/` (and whatever else Ruflo keeps as state) exists only in
    the main checkout, so a linked worktree's own root is the wrong cwd --
    every call there fails with "Database not initialized" and the mandatory
    Ruflo loop silently records nothing for worktree work.

    `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` answers the shared `.git` dir for both
    the main checkout and every linked worktree; its parent is the main
    checkout's own root. In the main checkout this IS `project` already (its
    `--git-common-dir` is just `.git`), so behaviour there is unchanged --
    only a linked worktree resolves somewhere else.

    Not a git repository at all, or the git call fails for any reason: falls
    back to `project` unchanged, same as `project_root`'s own fallback."""
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"],
                        capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=str(project))
    if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        return project
    common_dir = Path(r.stdout.strip())
    if not common_dir.is_absolute():
        common_dir = project / common_dir
    return common_dir.resolve().parent


def namespace_for(project: Path, explicit: str | None) -> str:
    """--namespace NS when given; else $RUFLO_NAMESPACE; else
    .common-rules.json's ruflo_namespace; else the project directory's own
    name. tools/ruflo.py does the actual resolution (RF-01), shared with
    bin/warmup and bin/conformance."""
    return RU.namespace_for(project, explicit)


def now_iso() -> str:
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def run_ruflo(binary: list[str], project: Path, *args: str) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    return subprocess.run([*binary, *args], cwd=str(project), capture_output=True, text=True)


def daemon_stop(binary: list[str] | None, project: Path) -> None:
    if binary is None:
        return
    subprocess.run([*binary, "daemon", "stop"], cwd=str(project), capture_output=True, text=True)


def _note_ignored(label: str, result: subprocess.CompletedProcess) -> None:
    """Best-effort telemetry, exactly like the app wrapper's `|| true`: a
    failed pre-task, route, post-task, list, search or testgaps-dispatch
    call is noted but never fails the item -- only `done`'s own gate, and
    the memory store calls below, do."""
    if result.returncode != 0:
        print(f"{PREFIX} {label} did not succeed (ignored): "
              f"{(result.stderr or result.stdout).strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
    elif result.stdout.strip():
        print(result.stdout.strip())


def _note_required(label: str, result: subprocess.CompletedProcess) -> bool:
    """Like `_note_ignored`, but for the `memory store` call that actually
    records `item:<ID>:start` / `item:<ID>:done` / `item:<ID>:note` --
    proposal 23 F-03: "an item without start and done in Ruflo memory is not
    done" only holds if a failed store is visible, not folded into the same
    best-effort `|| true` as pre-task/route/search telemetry. Returns True on
    success; the caller exits nonzero on False, one-line message on stderr,
    never silent success."""
    if result.returncode != 0:
        print(f"{PREFIX} {label} failed -- item not recorded in Ruflo memory: "
              f"{(result.stderr or result.stdout).strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
        return False
    if result.stdout.strip():
        print(result.stdout.strip())
    return True


def _terminate_process_group(proc: subprocess.Popen) -> None:
    """SIGTERM the whole group `proc` leads (it was started with
    start_new_session so it has one), escalating to SIGKILL after 5s if it
    will not go. Never pkill/killall -- this signals only the group of the
    one process ruflo-item itself started."""
    if proc.poll() is not None:
        return
    try:
        pgid = os.getpgid(proc.pid)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return
    try:
        os.killpg(pgid, signal.SIGTERM)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return
    try:
        proc.wait(timeout=5)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        try:
            os.killpg(pgid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except ProcessLookupError:
            pass
        proc.wait()


def _run_gate(gate: str, project: Path) -> int:
    """Runs the gate with inherited stdio (it streams live; only the exit
    code is used) in its own process group, so a SIGTERM/SIGHUP/Ctrl-C here
    can take the whole group down rather than orphaning it."""
    proc = subprocess.Popen(["bash", "-c", gate], cwd=str(project), start_new_session=True)
    try:
        return proc.wait()
    except (KeyboardInterrupt, _Signaled):
        _terminate_process_group(proc)
        raise


def _run_level_gate(level: str, project: Path, item_ids: list[str]) -> tuple[bool, bool]:
    """Return (green, ran_now) for the level-bound gate receipt."""
    gate = GR.command_for(project, level)
    if not gate:
        name = "quick" if level == "development" else "merge"
        print(f"{PREFIX} no {name} gate declared -- refusing", file=sys.stderr)
        return False, False
    reason = gate.split("#", 1)[1].strip() if gate.strip().startswith("false") and "#" in gate else None
    worktree = GR.fingerprint(project)
    prior = GR.reusable(project, level, gate, worktree)
    if prior:
        print(f"{PREFIX} {level} gate receipt reused for {', '.join(item_ids)} "
              f"(from {prior['level']} at {prior['at']})")
        return True, False
    print(f"{PREFIX} running {level} gate once for {', '.join(item_ids)}: {gate}")
    if _run_gate(gate, project) != 0:
        if reason:
            print(f"{PREFIX} gate refuses -- {reason}", file=sys.stderr)
        else:
            print(f"{PREFIX} {level} gate failed", file=sys.stderr)
        return False, True
    GR.record(project, level, gate, worktree)
    print(f"{PREFIX} {level} gate green")
    return True, True


def _split_item_flags(specs: list[str], parser: argparse.ArgumentParser) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Parse repeated `--item ID:TEXT` flags into [(id, text), ...], split on
    the *first* `:` only, so TEXT itself may contain colons. `parser.error()`
    (exit 2, matching argparse's own usage errors) on anything malformed, so
    a bad batch call fails before any Ruflo binary is even resolved."""
    items = []
    for spec in specs:
        if ":" not in spec:
            parser.error(f"--item {spec!r} is not ID:TEXT (missing ':')")
        item_id, text = spec.split(":", 1)
        item_id, text = item_id.strip(), text.strip()
        if not item_id or not text:
            parser.error(f"--item {spec!r} needs both a non-empty id and text")
        items.append((item_id, text))
    return items


def cmd_start(binary: list[str], project: Path, ruflo_cwd: Path,
              items: list[tuple[str, str]], ns: str) -> int:
    """items: [(id, task), ...] -- one id (today's shape) or several, each
    with its own task text (`--item ID:TEXT`, repeatable). One id failing its
    (required) memory store does not stop the rest of the batch; the call
    still returns nonzero if any id failed."""
    failed = []
    for item_id, task in items:
        print(f"{PREFIX} pre-task for {item_id}")
        _note_ignored("hooks pre-task", run_ruflo(
            binary, ruflo_cwd, "hooks", "pre-task", "--description", f"{item_id}: {task}"))

        print(f"{PREFIX} searched memory for {item_id!r} (namespace {ns})")
        _note_ignored("memory search", run_ruflo(
            binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "search", "--query", item_id, "--namespace", ns))

        print(f"{PREFIX} routed via hooks route --task")
        _note_ignored("hooks route", run_ruflo(
            binary, ruflo_cwd, "hooks", "route", "--task", f"{item_id}: {task}"))

        key = f"item:{item_id}:start"
        value = f"{now_iso()} {task}"
        if not _note_required("memory store", run_ruflo(
                binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "store", "--namespace", ns, "--key", key, "--value", value)):
            failed.append(item_id)
            continue
        print(f"{PREFIX} stored {key}")

    if failed:
        print(f"{PREFIX} start failed to record: {', '.join(failed)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0


def cmd_done(binary: list[str], project: Path, ruflo_cwd: Path,
             items: list[tuple[str, str]], ns: str) -> int:
    """items: [(id, summary), ...] -- either several ids sharing one summary
    (the positional `<ID> [<ID> ...] "<summary>"` form) or several ids each
    with their own summary (`--item ID:TEXT`, repeatable). The gate still
    runs exactly once for the whole call either way. One id's (required)
    memory store failing does not stop the rest of the batch from being
    recorded; the call returns nonzero if any id failed, and skips the final
    testgaps dispatch in that case -- unchanged from today's single-id
    behaviour, where a failed store already meant "no testgaps"."""
    item_ids = [item_id for item_id, _ in items]
    green, gate_ran = _run_level_gate("release", project, item_ids)
    if not green:
        return 1

    failed = []
    for item_id, summary in items:
        _note_ignored("hooks post-task", run_ruflo(
            binary, ruflo_cwd, "hooks", "post-task", "--task-id", item_id,
            "--success", "true", "--store-results", "true"))

        key = f"item:{item_id}:done"
        value = f"{now_iso()} {summary}"
        if not _note_required("memory store", run_ruflo(
                binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "store", "--namespace", ns, "--key", key, "--value", value)):
            failed.append(item_id)
            continue
        print(f"{PREFIX} stored {key}")

    if failed:
        print(f"{PREFIX} done failed to record: {', '.join(failed)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if gate_ran:
        _note_ignored("hooks worker dispatch", run_ruflo(
            binary, ruflo_cwd, "hooks", "worker", "dispatch", "--trigger", "testgaps"))
    return 0


def cmd_ready(binary: list[str], project: Path, ruflo_cwd: Path,
              items: list[tuple[str, str]], ns: str) -> int:
    """Focused development evidence: quick gate and ready memory, no worker."""
    item_ids = [item_id for item_id, _ in items]
    green, _gate_ran = _run_level_gate("development", project, item_ids)
    if not green:
        return 1
    failed = []
    for item_id, summary in items:
        key = f"item:{item_id}:ready"
        if not _note_required("memory store", run_ruflo(
                binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "store", "--namespace", ns,
                "--key", key, "--value", f"{now_iso()} {summary}")):
            failed.append(item_id)
        else:
            print(f"{PREFIX} stored {key}")
    return 1 if failed else 0


def cmd_checkpoint(binary: list[str], project: Path, ruflo_cwd: Path,
                   items: list[tuple[str, str]], ns: str, legacy_done: bool = False) -> int:
    """Feature-bundle checkpoint: merge gate once, memory, then testgaps."""
    item_ids = [item_id for item_id, _ in items]
    green, gate_ran = _run_level_gate("checkpoint", project, item_ids)
    if not green:
        return 1
    failed = []
    for item_id, summary in items:
        if legacy_done:
            _note_ignored("hooks post-task", run_ruflo(
                binary, ruflo_cwd, "hooks", "post-task", "--task-id", item_id,
                "--success", "true", "--store-results", "true"))
        suffixes = ("done",) if legacy_done else ("checkpoint", "done")
        for suffix in suffixes:
            key = f"item:{item_id}:{suffix}"
            if not _note_required("memory store", run_ruflo(
                    binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "store", "--namespace", ns,
                    "--key", key, "--value", f"{now_iso()} {summary}")):
                failed.append(item_id)
            else:
                print(f"{PREFIX} stored {key}")
    if failed:
        return 1
    if gate_ran:
        _note_ignored("hooks worker dispatch", run_ruflo(
            binary, ruflo_cwd, "hooks", "worker", "dispatch", "--trigger", "testgaps"))
    return 0


def cmd_note(binary: list[str], project: Path, ruflo_cwd: Path, item_id: str, text: str, ns: str) -> int:
    ts = int(datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).timestamp())
    key = f"item:{item_id}:note:{ts}"
    value = f"{now_iso()} {text}"
    if not _note_required("memory store", run_ruflo(
            binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "store", "--namespace", ns, "--key", key, "--value", value)):
        return 1
    print(f"{PREFIX} stored {key}")
    return 0


def cmd_recall(binary: list[str], project: Path, ruflo_cwd: Path, keywords: str, ns: str) -> int:
    print(f"{PREFIX} memory keys (namespace {ns}):")
    _note_ignored("memory list", run_ruflo(binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "list", "--namespace", ns))

    print(f"{PREFIX} searching memory for {keywords!r}")
    _note_ignored("memory search", run_ruflo(
        binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "search", "--query", keywords, "--namespace", ns))
    return 0


def _ledger_closed_items(ledger_path: Path, since) -> list[dict]:
    """Every row in `ledger_path` with status "done" whose `closed_date`
    (tools/tracker/ledger.py -- the same reading bin/conformance check 9
    uses) is on or after `since` (every done row, when `since` is None).
    Raises ValueError naming the file when it is not valid JSON."""
    data = LED.load(ledger_path)
    out = []
    for row in LED.items(data):
        if not isinstance(row, dict) or row.get("status") != "done" or not row.get("id"):
            continue
        date = LED.closed_date(row)
        if since is not None and (date is None or date < since):
            continue
        out.append(row)
    return out


def cmd_from_ledger(binary: list[str], project: Path, ruflo_cwd: Path,
                     ledger_path: Path, since, ns: str) -> int:
    """Backfills item:<ID>:start and item:<ID>:done into Ruflo memory for
    every item in `ledger_path` closed on or after `since` -- a memory
    *record* of history, not a re-run of it: no merge gate, no hooks
    pre-task/route/post-task, no testgaps dispatch (see the module docstring,
    "BATCHING", for why). Each stored value says plainly that it was
    backfilled and when, and names its original closed date. One id's
    (required) memory store failing does not stop the rest of the batch; the
    call returns nonzero if any id failed."""
    try:
        rows = _ledger_closed_items(ledger_path, since)
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(f"{PREFIX} {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if not rows:
        since_note = f" on or after {since.isoformat()}" if since else ""
        print(f"{PREFIX} no closed items in {ledger_path}{since_note} -- nothing to record")
        return 0

    backfilled_at = now_iso()
    failed = []
    for row in rows:
        item_id = row["id"]
        title = str(row.get("title") or item_id).strip() or item_id
        closed = LED.closed_date(row)
        closed_note = f"originally closed {closed.isoformat()}" if closed else "closed date unknown"
        note = (f"{title} -- backfilled {backfilled_at} from {ledger_path.name}, {closed_note}; "
                f"recorded after the fact, not logged as the work happened")

        print(f"{PREFIX} backfilling {item_id} from {ledger_path.name}")
        start_key = f"item:{item_id}:start"
        if not _note_required("memory store", run_ruflo(
                binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "store", "--namespace", ns,
                "--key", start_key, "--value", f"{backfilled_at} {note}")):
            failed.append(item_id)
            continue
        print(f"{PREFIX} stored {start_key}")

        done_key = f"item:{item_id}:done"
        if not _note_required("memory store", run_ruflo(
                binary, ruflo_cwd, "memory", "store", "--namespace", ns,
                "--key", done_key, "--value", f"{backfilled_at} {note}")):
            failed.append(item_id)
            continue
        print(f"{PREFIX} stored {done_key}")

    if failed:
        print(f"{PREFIX} from-ledger failed to record: {', '.join(sorted(set(failed)))}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="ruflo-item", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("--project", help="the project root (default: git rev-parse --show-toplevel of cwd)")
    ap.add_argument("--namespace", help="the Ruflo memory namespace "
                                         "(default: $RUFLO_NAMESPACE, else the project directory name)")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    p_start = sub.add_parser("start")
    p_start.add_argument("args", nargs="*", metavar="ID TASK",
                          help="an id and its task text (today's shape)")
    p_start.add_argument("--item", action="append", default=[], metavar="ID:TEXT",
                          help="a batch id and its own task text; repeatable, split on the first "
                               "':'. Mutually exclusive with the positional ID TASK form. Example: "
                               "start --item O-11:'wire it up' --item O-12:'stamp workflow.html'")

    completion_parsers = {}
    for name in ("ready", "checkpoint", "land", "done"):
        p = sub.add_parser(name, help=("compatibility alias for checkpoint" if name == "done" else None))
        p.add_argument("args", nargs="*", metavar="ID [ID ...] SUMMARY",
                       help="one or more item ids followed by one summary shared by all of them")
        p.add_argument("--item", action="append", default=[], metavar="ID:TEXT",
                       help="a batch id and its own summary; repeatable, split on the first ':'")
        completion_parsers[name] = p

    p_note = sub.add_parser("note")
    p_note.add_argument("id")
    p_note.add_argument("text")

    p_recall = sub.add_parser("recall")
    p_recall.add_argument("keywords")

    p_from_ledger = sub.add_parser("from-ledger")
    p_from_ledger.add_argument("ledger", type=Path, help="the ledger JSON file, e.g. "
                                "docs/proposals/31-....json")
    p_from_ledger.add_argument("--since", metavar="YYYY-MM-DD",
                                help="only items closed on or after this date (default: every done item)")

    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    # Batch-shape validation happens before any Ruflo binary is resolved --
    # a malformed call should fail fast, not after a cold start.
    start_items = completion_items = since = None
    if args.cmd == "start":
        if args.item:
            if args.args:
                p_start.error("pass either ID TASK or one or more --item ID:TEXT, not both")
            start_items = _split_item_flags(args.item, p_start)
        else:
            if len(args.args) != 2:
                p_start.error("need exactly ID and TASK, or one or more --item ID:TEXT")
            start_items = [(args.args[0], args.args[1])]
    elif args.cmd in completion_parsers:
        parser = completion_parsers[args.cmd]
        if args.item:
            if args.args:
                parser.error("pass either ID... SUMMARY or one or more --item ID:TEXT, not both")
            completion_items = _split_item_flags(args.item, parser)
        else:
            if len(args.args) < 2:
                parser.error("need one or more ids and a shared SUMMARY, or one or more --item ID:TEXT")
            *ids, summary = args.args
            completion_items = [(item_id, summary) for item_id in ids]
    elif args.cmd == "from-ledger":
        if args.since:
            try:
                since = datetime.date.fromisoformat(args.since)
            except ValueError:
                p_from_ledger.error(f"--since {args.since!r} is not YYYY-MM-DD")

    binary, err = resolve_binary()
    if binary is None:
        print(err, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print(f"{PREFIX} binary: {' '.join(binary)}")

    project = project_root(args.project)
    ruflo_cwd = ruflo_cwd_for(project)
    ns = namespace_for(project, args.namespace)
    print(f"{PREFIX} namespace: {ns}")

    _install_signal_handlers()
    try:
        if args.cmd == "start":
            return cmd_start(binary, project, ruflo_cwd, start_items, ns)
        if args.cmd == "ready":
            return cmd_ready(binary, project, ruflo_cwd, completion_items, ns)
        if args.cmd in ("checkpoint", "done"):
            return cmd_checkpoint(binary, project, ruflo_cwd, completion_items, ns,
                                  legacy_done=args.cmd == "done")
        if args.cmd == "land":
            return cmd_done(binary, project, ruflo_cwd, completion_items, ns)
        if args.cmd == "note":
            return cmd_note(binary, project, ruflo_cwd, args.id, args.text, ns)
        if args.cmd == "recall":
            return cmd_recall(binary, project, ruflo_cwd, args.keywords, ns)
        if args.cmd == "from-ledger":
            return cmd_from_ledger(binary, project, ruflo_cwd, args.ledger, since, ns)
        return 2  # pragma: no cover -- argparse's `required=True` rules this out
    except _Signaled as sig:
        return 128 + sig.signum
    finally:
        daemon_stop(binary, ruflo_cwd)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
